      "title": "Beau Lamarre-Condon told friend if he killed anyone he’d dump belongings in bin and take bodies to bushland, court hears",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/sep/28/beau-lamarre-condon-murder-trial-court-ntwnfb",
      "description": "<p>Former police officer has pleaded not guilty to murdering Jesse Baird and Luke Davies in early 2024 – nine months after discussion with friend Georgia Cook</p><ul><li><p><a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2026/sep/28/labor-anthony-albanese-openai-ai-hacks-coalition-one-nation-medicare-budget-ntwnfb\">Follow our Australia news live blog for latest updates</a></p></li><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">new political email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>A Sydney police officer told a friend he would hide the bodies of anyone he killed in bushland and put their belongings in a dumpster, months before allegedly murdering Jesse Baird and Luke Davies, a court has been told.</p><p>Beau Lamarre-Condon, 31, has been accused of fatally shooting the couple with a police service weapon in Baird’s Paddington home on 19 February 2024.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/sep/28/beau-lamarre-condon-murder-trial-court-ntwnfb\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-09-28T01:15:31"
    },
    {
      "title": "Shannon felt a ‘sense of doom’ as she sought justice for sexual assault. A new NSW program hopes to alleviate that",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/sep/28/sexual-assault-service-nsw-victim-survivor-opening-ntwnfb",
      "description": "<p><strong>Exclusive: </strong>Dedicated service to support sexual assault victim-survivors as they navigate legal system to open in NSW</p><ul><li><p><a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2026/sep/28/labor-anthony-albanese-openai-ai-hacks-coalition-one-nation-medicare-budget-ntwnfb\">Follow our Australia news live blog for latest updates</a></p></li><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">breaking news email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>It took Shannon Stokes two years and 11 days to successfully prosecute her former boyfriend for sexual assault and domestic violence offences. The lengthy trial left her feeling as though “there wasn’t going to be an end in sight”.</p><p>Prosecutors secured convictions on nine of the 11 charges, and he was later sentenced to five years in prison.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/sep/28/sexual-assault-service-nsw-victim-survivor-opening-ntwnfb\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-09-27T20:00:13"
    },
    {
      "title": "Republican senator criticizes White House for taxpayer-funded Trump ads",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/sep/27/john-kennedy-white-house-trump-ad-tax-money",
      "description": "<p>John Kennedy pushes back against such spending as pro-Trump ‘public service announcements’ air before midterms</p><p>As the White House expands a taxpayer-funded advertising campaign to promote <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump\">Donald Trump</a>’s political agenda before the <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/us-midterm-elections-2026\">November midterm elections</a>, US senator John Kennedy <a href=\"https://youtu.be/xcAm_R6UfCw?si=cxz_VwC6CqZVCWhH&amp;t=385\">told CBS News</a> on Sunday “there ought to be a rule” against such spending – before tacitly admitting such spending is “probably” illegal.</p><p>Kennedy, the US president’s fellow <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/republicans\">Republican</a>, was asked by Margaret Brennan, the CBS Face the Nation host, about the White House spending to air multiple <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/sep/25/white-house-pro-trump-ad-fox\">ads featuring Trump</a> – including one that was produced for his 2024 election campaign.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/sep/27/john-kennedy-white-house-trump-ad-tax-money\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-09-27T23:40:34"
    },
    {
      "title": "Blanche defends Trump’s White House media ban: ‘It is a privilege, not a right’",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/sep/27/todd-blanche-trump-white-house-media",
      "description": "<p>US attorney general argues that president was ‘sick and tired’ of inaccurate reporting by CNN, MS Now and Politico</p><p><a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump\">Donald Trump</a>’s attorney general Todd Blanche argued on Sunday that the administration was permitted to<strong> </strong>ban three news outlets from the White House because “it is a privilege and not a right to be there”.</p><p>Appearing on the ABC News program This Week during the second week of the White House’s dispute over press credentialing and access, Blanche also said that the administration took the decision to ban CNN, MS Now and Politico beginning on 19 September because the president was “sick and tired” of what he considered to be those media outlets’ inaccurate reporting.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/sep/27/todd-blanche-trump-white-house-media\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-09-27T21:42:56"
    },
    {
